(Sampson Awdeley,--for by all these names, says Mr Payne Collier, was our) Tj T*
(one man known:--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    It may be disputed whether this printer's name were really Sampson,) Tj T*
(    or Awdeley: he was made free of the Stationers' Company as Sampson,) Tj T*
(    and so he is most frequently termed towards the commencement of the) Tj T*
(    Register; but he certainly wrote and printed his name Awdeley or) Tj T*
(    Awdelay; now and then it stands in the Register 'Sampson Awdeley.') Tj T*
(    It is the more important to settle the point, because ... he was not) Tj T*
(    only a printer, but a versifier,[2] and ought to have been included) Tj T*
(    by Ritson in his _Bibliographica Poetica_. \(Registers of the) Tj T*
(    Stationers' Company, A.D. 1848, vol. i. p. 23.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These verses of Awdeley's, or Sampson's, no doubt led to his 'small) Tj T*
(bréefe' being entered in the Stationers' Register as a 'ballett':) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  "1560-1. Rd. of John Sampson, for his lycense for pryntinge of a) Tj T*
(  ballett called the description of vakaboundes ... iiij^{d}.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    "[This entry seems to refer to an early edition of a very curious) Tj T*
(    work, printed again by Sampson, alias Awdeley, in 1565, when it bore) Tj T*
(    the following title, 'The fraternitie of vacabondes, as well of) Tj T*
(    rufling vacabones as of beggerly, [3]as well of women as of men,) Tj T*
(    [3]and as well of gyrles as of boyes, with their proper names and) Tj T*
(    qualityes. Also the xxv. orders of knaves, otherwise called a) Tj T*
(    quartten of knawes. Confirmed this yere by Cocke Lorel.' The edition) Tj T*
(    without date mentioned by Dibdin \(iv. 564\) may have been that of the) Tj T*
(    entry. Another impression by Awdeley, dated 1575 [which we reprint]) Tj T*
(    is reviewed in the _British Bibliographer_, ii. 12, where it is) Tj T*
(    asserted \(as is very probable, though we are without distinct) Tj T*
(    evidence of the fact\) that the printer was the compiler of the book,) Tj T*
(    and he certainly introduces it by three six-line stanzas. If this) Tj T*
(    work came out originally in 1561, according to the entry, there is) Tj T*
(    no doubt that it was the precursor of a very singular series of) Tj T*
(    tracts on the same subject, which will be noticed in their proper) Tj T*
(    places.]"--J. P. Collier, _Registers_, i. 42.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As above said, I take Harman's 'fewe yeares'--in 1566 or 7--to point to) Tj T*
(the 1561 edition of Awdeley, and not the 1565 ed. And as to Awdeley's) Tj T*
(authorship,--what can be more express than his own words, p. 2, below,) Tj T*
(that what the Vagabond caught at a Session confest as to 'both names and) Tj T*
(states of most and least of this their Vacabondes brotherhood,') Tj T*
(_that_,--'at the request of a worshipful man, I ['The Printer,' that is,) Tj T*
(John Awdeley] have set it forth as well as I can.') Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But if a doubt on Awdeley's priority to Harman exists in any reader's) Tj T*
(mind, let him consider this second reference by Harman to Awdeley \(p.) Tj T*
(60, below\), not noticed by the bibliographers: "For-as-much as these two) Tj T*
(names, a Iarkeman and a Patrico, bée in _the old briefe of vacabonds_,) Tj T*
(and set forth as two kyndes of euil doers, you shall vnderstande that a) Tj T*
(Iarkeman hath his name of a _Iarke, which is a seale in their Language_,) Tj T*
(as one should _make writinges and set seales for lycences_ and) Tj T*
