() Tj T*
(We may conclude, then, certainly, that Awdeley did not plagiarize) Tj T*
(Harman; and probably, that he first published his _Fraternitye_ in 1561.) Tj T*
(The tract is a mere sketch, as compared with Harman's _Caueat_, though) Tj T*
(in its descriptions \(p. 6-11\) of 'A Curtesy Man,' 'A Cheatour or) Tj T*
(Fingerer,' and 'A Ring-Faller' \(one of whom tried his tricks on me in) Tj T*
(Gower-street about ten days ago\), it gives as full a picture as Harman) Tj T*
(does of the general run of his characters. The edition of 1575 being the) Tj T*
(only one accessible to us, our trusty Oxford copier, Mr George Parker,) Tj T*
(has read the proofs with the copy in the Bodleian.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Let no one bring a charge of plagiarizing Awdeley, against Harman, for) Tj T*
(the latter, as has been shown, referred fairly to Awdeley's '_small) Tj T*
(breefe_' or '_old briefe of vacabonds_,' and wrote his own "bolde) Tj T*
(Beggars booke" \(p. 91\) from his own long experience with them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
