(take a long extract from his said chapter. After speaking of those who) Tj T*
(are made 'beggers through other mens occasion,' and denouncing the) Tj T*
(grasping landlords 'who make them so, and wipe manie out of their) Tj T*
(occupiengs,' Harrison goes on to those who are beggars 'through their) Tj T*
(owne default' \(p. 183, last line of col. 1, ed. 1586\):) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    "Such as are idle beggers through their owne default are of two) Tj T*
(    sorts, and continue their estates either by casuall or meere) Tj T*
(    voluntarie meanes: those that are such by casuall means [16]are in) Tj T*
(    the beginning[16] iustlie to be referred either to the first or) Tj T*
(    second sort of poore [16]afore mentioned[16]; but, degenerating into) Tj T*
(    the thriftlesse sort, they doo what they can to continue their) Tj T*
(    miserie; and, with such impediments as they haue, to straie and) Tj T*
(    wander about, as creatures abhorring all labour and euerie honest) Tj T*
(    excercise. Certes, I call these casuall meanes, not in respect of) Tj T*
(    the originall of their pouertie, but of the continuance of the same,) Tj T*
