([Header: _THE GROUNDWORKE OF CONNY-CATCHING_, 1592.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among the users of Harman's book, the chief and coolest was the author) Tj T*
(of _The groundworke of Conny-catching_, 1592, who wrote a few) Tj T*
(introductory pages, and then quietly reprinted almost all Harman's book) Tj T*
(with an 'I leaue you now vnto those which by Maister Harman are) Tj T*
(discouered' \(p. 103, below\). By this time Harman was no doubt dead.--Who) Tj T*
(will search for his Will in the Wills Office?--Though Samuel Rowlands) Tj T*
(was alive, he did not show up this early appropriator of Harman's work) Tj T*
(as he did a later one. As a kind of Supplement to the _Caueat_, I have) Tj T*
(added, as the 4th tract in the present volume, such parts of the) Tj T*
(_Groundworke of Conny-catching_ as are not reprinted from Harman. The) Tj T*
(_Groundworke_ has been attributed to Robert Greene, but on no evidence) Tj T*
(\(I believe\) except Greene's having written a book in three Parts on) Tj T*
(Conny-catching, 1591-2, and 'A Disputation betweene a Hee Conny-catcher) Tj T*
(and a Shee Conny-catcher, whether a Theafe or a Whore is most hvrtfull) Tj T*
(in Cousonage to the Common-wealth,' 1592.[33] Hearne's copy of the) Tj T*
(_Groundworke_ is bound up in the 2nd vol. of Greene's Works, among) Tj T*
(George III.'s books in the British Museum, as if it really was Greene's.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another pilferer from Harman was Thomas Dekker, in his _Belman of) Tj T*
(London_, 1608, of which three editions were published in the same year) Tj T*
(\(_Hazlitt_\). But Samuel Rowlands found him out and showed him up. From) Tj T*
(the fifth edition of the Belman, the earliest that our copier, Mr W. M.) Tj T*
(Wood, could find in the British Museum, he has drawn up the following) Tj T*
(account of the book:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    _The Belman of London. Bringing to Light the most notorious) Tj T*
(        Villanies that are now practised in the Kingdome. Profitable for) Tj T*
(        Gentlemen, Lawyers, Merchants, Citizens, Farmers, Masters of) Tj T*
(        Housholds, and all sorts of Servants to mark, and delightfull) Tj T*
(        for all Men to Reade._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    Lege, Perlege, Relege.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
