(    'Martin Mark-all, his Apologie to the Bel-man of London. There hath) Tj T*
(    been of late dayes great paines taken on the part of the good old) Tj T*
(    Bel-man of London, in discouering, as hee thinks, a new-found Nation) Tj T*
(    and People. Let it be so for this time: hereupon much adoe was made) Tj T*
(    in setting forth their lines, order of lining, method of speech, and) Tj T*
(    vsuall meetings, with diuers other things thereunto appertaining.) Tj T*
(    These volumes and papers, now spread euerie where, so that euerie) Tj T*
(    Iacke-boy now can say as well as the proudest of that fraternitie,) Tj T*
(    "will you wapp for a wyn, or tranie for a make?" The gentle Company) Tj T*
(    of Cursitours began now to stirre, and looke about them; and hauing) Tj T*
(    gathered together a Conuocation of Canting Caterpillars, as wel in) Tj T*
(    the North parts at the Diuels arse apeake,[35] as in the South, they) Tj T*
(    diligently enquired, and straight search was made, whether any had) Tj T*
(    reuolted from that faithles fellowship. Herupon euery one gaue his) Tj T*
(    verdict: some supposed that it might be some one that, hauing) Tj T*
(    ventured to farre beyond wit and good taking heede, was fallen into) Tj T*
(    the hands of the Magistrate, and carried to the trayning Cheates,) Tj T*
(    where, in shew of a penitent heart, and remoarse of his good time) Tj T*
(    ill spent, turned the cocke, and let out all: others thought it) Tj T*
(    might be some spic-knaue that, hauing little to doe, tooke vpon him) Tj T*
(    the habite and forme of an Hermite; and so, by dayly commercing and) Tj T*
(    discoursing, learned in time the mysterie and knowlege of this) Tj T*
(    ignoble profession: and others, because it smelt of a study, deemed) Tj T*
(    it to be some of their owne companie, that had been at some) Tj T*
(    free-schoole, and belike, because hee would be handsome against a) Tj T*
(    good time, tooke pen and inke, and wrote of that subiect; thus, _Tot) Tj T*
(    homines, tot sententiæ_, so many men, so many mindes. And all) Tj T*
(    because the spightfull Poet would not set too his name. At last vp) Tj T*
(    starts an old Cacodemicall Academicke with his frize bonnet, and) Tj T*
(    giues them al to know, that this invectiue was set foorth, made, and) Tj T*
(    printed Fortie yeeres agoe. And being then called, 'A caueat for) Tj T*
(    Cursitors,' is now newly printed, and termed, 'The Bel-man of) Tj T*
(    London,' made at first by one Master Harman, a Iustice of Peace in) Tj T*
(    Kent, in Queene Marie's daies,--he being then about ten yeeres of) Tj T*
(    age.' Sign. A. 2.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    'They \(the vagabonds\) haue a language among themselues, composed of) Tj T*
(    _omnium gatherum_; a glimering whereof, one of late daies hath) Tj T*
(    endeuoured to manifest, as farre as his Authour is pleased to be an) Tj T*
(    intelligencer. The substance whereof he leaueth for those that will) Tj T*
(    dilate thereof; enough for him to haue the praise, other the paines,) Tj T*
(    notwithstanding _Harman's_ ghost continually clogging his conscience) Tj T*
(    with _Sic Vos non Vobis_.'--Sign. C. 3 back.[36]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    'Because the Bel-man entreateth any that is more rich in canting, to) Tj T*
