(breake my mind vnto your good maistership. Thus it is syr, ther is a) Tj T*
(certaine of vs \(though I say it both taule and handsome men of theyr) Tj T*
(hands\) which haue come lately from the wars, and as God knoweth haue) Tj T*
(nothing to take to, being both maisterles and moniles, & knowing no way) Tj T*
(wherby to yerne one peny. And further, wher as we haue bene welthely) Tj T*
(brought vp, and we also haue beene had in good estimatio_n_, we are a) Tj T*
(shamed now to declare our misery, and to fall a crauing as common) Tj T*
(Beggers, and as for to steale and robbe, \(God is our record\) it striketh) Tj T*
(vs to *[leaf 4]* the hart, to thinke of such a mischiefe, that euer any) Tj T*
(handsome man should fall into such a daunger for thys worldly trash.) Tj T*
(Which if we had to suffise our want and necessity, we should neuer seeke) Tj T*
(thus shamefastly to craue on such good pityfull men as you seeme to be,) Tj T*
(neither yet so daungerously to hasarde our liues for so vyle a thing.) Tj T*
(Therefore good syr, as you seeme to be a handsome man your selfe, and) Tj T*
(also such a one as pitieth the miserable case of handsome men, as now) Tj T*
(your eyes and countenaunce sheweth to haue some pity vppon this my) Tj T*
(miserable complainte: So in Gods cause I require your maistershyp, & in) Tj T*
(the behalfe of my poore afflicted fellowes, which though here in sight) Tj T*
(they cry not with me to you, yet wheresouer they bee, I am sure they cry) Tj T*
(vnto God to moue the heartes of some good men to shew forth their) Tj T*
(liberality in this behalfe. All which & I with them craue now the same) Tj T*
(request at your good masterships hand. With these or such like words he) Tj T*
(frameth his talke. Now if the party \(which he thus talketh withall\)) Tj T*
(profereth hym a peny or .ii.d. he taketh it, but verye scornfully, and) Tj T*
(at last speaketh on this sorte: Well syr, your good will is not to be) Tj T*
(refused. But yet you shall vnderstand \(good syr\) that this is nothing) Tj T*
(for them, for whom I do thus shamefastly entreate. Alas syr, it is not a) Tj T*
(groate or .xii.d. I speake for, being such a company of Seruiters as wee) Tj T*
(haue bene: yet neuertheles God forbid I should not receiue your ge_n_tle) Tj T*
(offer at this time, hoping hereafter through your good motions to some) Tj T*
(such lyke good gentleman as you be, that I, or some of my fellowes in my) Tj T*
(place, shall finde the more liberality. These kind of ydle Vacabondes) Tj T*
(wyll go commonly well appareled, without *[leaf 4b.]* any weapon, and in) Tj T*
(place where they meete together, as at their hosteryes or other places,) Tj T*
(they wyll beare the port of ryght good gentlemen, & some are the more) Tj T*
(trusted, but co_m_monly thei pay them w_i_t_h_ stealing a paire of) Tj T*
(sheetes, or Couerlet, & so take their farewell earely in the morning,) Tj T*
(before the mayster or dame be sturring.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A CHEATOUR OR FINGERER.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These commonly be such kinde of idle Vacabondes as scarcely a man shall) Tj T*
(discerne, they go so gorgeously, sometime with waiting men, and sometime) Tj T*
(without. Their trade is to walke in such places, where as gentelmen &) Tj T*
(other worshipfull Citizens do resorte, as at Poules, or at Christes) Tj T*
(Hospital, & somtime at ye Royal exchaunge. These haue very many) Tj T*
(acquaintaunces, yea, and for the most part will acquaint them selues) Tj T*
(with euery man, and fayne a society, in one place or other. But chiefly) Tj T*
(they wil seeke their acquaintaunce of such \(which they haue learned by) Tj T*
(diligent enquiring where they resort\) as haue receyued some porcioun of) Tj T*
(money of their friends, as yong Gentlemen which are sent to London to) Tj T*
(study the lawes, or els some yong Marchant man or other kynde of) Tj T*
ET
