(Occupier, whose friendes hath geuen them a stock of mony[41] to occupy) Tj T*
(withall. When they haue thus found out such a pray, they will find the) Tj T*
(meanes by theyr familiarity, as very curteously to bid him to breakefast) Tj T*
(at one place or other, where they are best acquainted, and closely) Tj T*
(amonge themselues wil appoint one of their Fraternity, which they call a) Tj T*
(Fyngerer, an olde beaten childe, not onely in such deceites, but also) Tj T*
(such a one as by his age is painted out with gray heares, wrinkled face,) Tj T*
(crooked back, and most commonly lame, as it might seeme with age, *[leaf) Tj T*
(5]* yea and such a one as to shew a simplicity, shal weare a homely) Tj T*
(cloke and hat scarce worth .vi. d. This nimble fingred knight \(being) Tj T*
(appointed to this place\) co_m_meth in as one not knowen of these) Tj T*
(Cheatours, but as vnwares shal sit down at the end of the bord where) Tj T*
(they syt, & call for his peny pot of wine, or a pinte of Ale, as the) Tj T*
(place serueth. Thus sitting as it were alone, mumblyng on a crust, or) Tj T*
(some such thing, these other yonckers wil finde some kind of mery talke) Tj T*
(with him, some times questioning wher he dwelleth, & sometimes enquiring) Tj T*
(what trade he vseth, which co_m_monly he telleth them he vseth) Tj T*
(husbandry: & talking thus merely, at last they aske him, how sayest) Tj T*
(thou, Father, wylt thou play for thy breakfast with one of vs, that we) Tj T*
(may haue some pastime as we syt? Thys olde Karle makyng it straunge at) Tj T*
(the first saith: My maysters, ich am an old man, and halfe blinde, and) Tj T*
(can skyl of very few games, yet for that you seeme to be such good) Tj T*
(Gentelmen, as to profer to play for that of which you had no part, but) Tj T*
(onely I my selfe, and therefore of right ich am worthy to pay for it, I) Tj T*
(shal with al my hart fulfyl your request. And so falleth to play,) Tj T*
(somtime at Cardes & sometime at dice. Which through his cou_n_terfait) Tj T*
(simplicity in the play somtimes ouer counteth himself, or playeth) Tj T*
(somtimes against his wyl, so as he would not, & then counterfaiteth to) Tj T*
(be angry, and falleth to swearing, & so leesing that, profereth to play) Tj T*
(for a shillyng or two. The other therat hauing good sport, seming to) Tj T*
(mocke him, falleth againe to play, and so by their legerdemane, &) Tj T*
(cou_n_terfaiting, winneth ech of them a shilling or twain, & at last) Tj T*
(whispereth the yong man in the eare to play with hym also, that ech one) Tj T*
(might haue a fling at him. *[leaf 5b.]* This yong ma_n_ for company) Tj T*
(falleth againe to play also with the sayd Fyngerer, and winneth as the) Tj T*
(other did which when he had loste a noble or .vi. s. maketh as though he) Tj T*
(had lost al his mony, and falleth a intreating for parte thereof againe) Tj T*
(to bring him home, which the other knowing his mind and intent, stoutely) Tj T*
(denieth and iesteth, & scoffeth at him. This Fingerer seeming then to be) Tj T*
(in a rage, desireth the_m_ as they are true gentlemen, to tarry till he) Tj T*
(fetcheth more store of money, or els to point some place where they may) Tj T*
(meete. They seeming greedy hereof, promiseth faithfully and clappeth) Tj T*
(handes so to meete. They thus ticklyng the young man in the eare,) Tj T*
(willeth him to make as much money as he can, and they wil make as much) Tj T*
(as they can, and co_n_sent as though they wil play booty against him.) Tj T*
(But in the ende they so vse the matter, that both the young man leeseth) Tj T*
(his part, and, as it seemeth to him, they leesing theirs also, and so) Tj T*
(maketh as though they would fal together by the eares with this) Tj T*
(fingerer, which by one wyle or other at last conueyeth him selfe away, &) Tj T*
(they as it were raging lyke mad bedlams, one runneth one way, an other) Tj T*
(an other way, leauing the loser indeede all alone. Thus these Cheatours) Tj T*
(at their accustomed hosteries meete closely together, and there receiue) Tj T*
