(¶ A RING FALLER.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A Ryng faller is he that getteth fayre copper rings, some made like) Tj T*
(signets, & some after other fashio_n_s, very faire gylded, & walketh vp) Tj T*
(and down the streetes, til he spieth some man of the country, or some) Tj T*
(other simple body whom he thinketh he may deceaue, and so goeth a lyttle) Tj T*
(before him or them, and letteth fall one of these ringes, which when the) Tj T*
(party that commeth after spieth and taketh it vp, he hauing an eye) Tj T*
(backward, crieth halfe part, the party that taketh it vp, thinking it to) Tj T*
(be of great value, profereth him some money for his part, which he not) Tj T*
(fully denieth, but willeth him to come into some alehouse or tauerne,) Tj T*
(and there they will common vpon the matter. Which when they come in, and) Tj T*
(are set in some solitary place \(as commonly they call for such a place\)) Tj T*
(there he desireth the party that found the ring to shew it him. When he) Tj T*
(seeth it, he falleth a entreating the party that found it, and desireth) Tj T*
(him to take money for his part, and telleth him that if euer he may do) Tj T*
(him any frendship hereafter he shal commaund him, for he maketh as) Tj T*
(though he were very desirous to haue it. The symple man seeing him so) Tj T*
(importune vpon it, thinketh the ring to bee of great valure, and so is) Tj T*
(the more lother to part from it. At last this ring faller asketh him) Tj T*
(what he will geue him for his part, for, saith he, seeing you wyl not) Tj T*
(let me haue the ring, alowe me my part, and take you the ring. The other) Tj T*
(asketh what he counteth the ring to be worth, he answereth, v. or vi.) Tj T*
(pound. No, saith he, it is not so much worth. *[leaf 6b.]* Well \(saith) Tj T*
(this Ringfaller\) let me haue it, and I wyll alow you .xl. s. for your) Tj T*
(part. The other party standyng in a doubt, and looking on the ryng,) Tj T*
(asketh if he wyll geue the money out of hand. The other answereth, he) Tj T*
(hath not so much ready mony about him, but he wil go fetch so much for) Tj T*
(him, if he wil go with him. The other that found the ring, thinking he) Tj T*
(meaneth truly, beginneth to profer him .xx. s. for his part, sometymes) Tj T*
(more, or les, which he verye scornfullye refuseth at the first, and styl) Tj T*
(entreateth that he might haue the ring, which maketh the other more) Tj T*
(fonder of it, and desireth him to take the money for his part, & so) Tj T*
(profereth him money. This ring faller seing y^{e} mony, maketh it very) Tj T*
(strau_n_ge, and first questioneth with him wher he dwelleth, and asketh) Tj T*
(him what is his name, & telleth him that he semeth to be an honest man,) Tj T*
(and therfore he wil do somwhat for friendships sake, hoping to haue as) Tj T*
(friendly a pleasure at his hand hereafter, and so profereth hym for .x.) Tj T*
(s. more he should haue the ryng. At last, with entreatye on both partes,) Tj T*
(he geueth the Ring faller the money, and so departeth, thinkyng he hath) Tj T*
(gotten a very great Iewell. These kynde of deceyuing Vacabondes haue) Tj T*
(other practises with their rings, as somtimes to come to buy wares of) Tj T*
