(Canting--began but within these xxx. yeeres,[50] lytle aboue; and that) Tj T*
(the first inuenter therof was hanged, all saue the head; for that is the) Tj T*
(fynall end of them all, or els to dye of some filthy and horyble) Tj T*
(diseases: but much harme is don in the meane space by their continuance,) Tj T*
(as some x., xii., and xvi. yeares before they be consumed, and the) Tj T*
(number of them doth dayly renew. I hope their synne is now at the) Tj T*
(hyghest; and that as short and as spedy a redresse wylbe for these, as) Tj T*
(hath bene of late yeres for _th_e wretched, wily, wandering vagabonds) Tj T*
(calling and naming them selues Egiptians, depely dissembling and long) Tj T*
(hyding _and_ couering their depe, decetfull practises,--feding the rude) Tj T*
(common people, wholy addicted and geuen to nouelties, toyes, and new) Tj T*
(inuentions,--delyting them with the strangenes of the attyre of their) Tj T*
(heades, and practising paulmistrie to such as would know their fortunes:) Tj T*
(And, to be short, all theues and hores \(as I may well wryt\),--as some) Tj T*
(haue had true experience, a number can well wytnes, and a great sorte) Tj T*
(hath well felte it. And now \(thankes bée to god\), throughe wholsome) Tj T*
(lawes, and the due execution thereof, all be dispersed, banished,[51]) Tj T*
