(AL though, good Reader, I wright in plain termes--and not so playnly as) Tj T*
(truely--concerning the matter, meaning honestly to all men, and wyshe) Tj T*
(them as much good as to myne owne harte; yet, as there hathe bene, so) Tj T*
(there is nowe, and hereafter wylbe, curyous heds to finde fauttes:) Tj T*
(wherefore I thought it necessary, now at this seconde Impression, to) Tj T*
(acquaynt _th_e with a great faulte, as some takethe it, but none[53] as) Tj T*
(I meane it, callinge these Vagabonds Cursetors in the intytelynge of my) Tj T*
(booke, as runneres or rangers aboute the countrey, deriued of this Laten) Tj T*
(word \(_Curro_\): neither do I wryght it Cooresetores, with a duble[54]) Tj T*
(oo; or Cowresetors, with a w, which hath an other singnification: is) Tj T*
(there no deuersite betwen a gardein and a garden, maynteynaunce _and_) Tj T*
(maintenance, Streytes and stretes? those that haue vnderstanding knowe) Tj T*
(there is a great dyfference: who is so ignorant by these dayes as) Tj T*
(knoweth not the meaning of a vagabone? and yf an ydell leuterar should) Tj T*
(be so called of eny man, would not he thi_n_k it bothe odyous and) Tj T*
(reprochefull? wyll he not shonne the name? ye, and where as he maye and) Tj T*
(dare, w_i_t_h_ bent browes, wyll reueng that name of Ingnomy: yet this) Tj T*
(playne name vagabone is deryued, as others be, of Laten wordes, and now) Tj T*
(vse makes it commen to al men; but let vs loke back four .C. yeres) Tj T*
(sithens, _and_ let vs se whether this playn word vagabon was vsed or no.) Tj T*
(I beleue not, and why? because I rede of no such name in the old) Tj T*
(estatutes of this realme, vnles it be in the margente of the booke, or) Tj T*
(in the Table, which in the collection and pryntinge was set in; but) Tj T*
(these were then the co_m_men names of these leud leuterars, Faytores,) Tj T*
(Robardesmen, Drawlatches, _and_ valyant beggares. Yf I should haue vsed) Tj T*
(suche wordes, or the same order of wryting, as this realme vsed in Kynge) Tj T*
(Henry the thyrd or Edward _th_e fyrstes tyme, oh, what a grose,) Tj T*
(barberous fellow *[leaf 5, back]* haue we here! his wryting is both) Tj T*
(homely and darke, that wee had nede to haue an interpretar: yet then it) Tj T*
(was verye well, and in short season a great change we see. well, this) Tj T*
(delycat age shall haue his tyme on the other syde. Eloquence haue I) Tj T*
(none; I neuer was acquaynted with the muses; I neuer tasted of Helycon.) Tj T*
(But accordinge to my playne order, I haue setforth this worke, symplye) Tj T*
(and truelye, with such vsual words and termes as is among vs wel known) Tj T*
(and frequented. So that as _th_e prouerbe saythe, "all though truth be) Tj T*
(blamed, it shal neuer be shamed." well, good reader, I meane not to be) Tj T*
(tedyous vnto the, but haue added fyue or sixe more tales, because some) Tj T*
(of them weare donn whyle my booke was fyrste in the presse; and as I) Tj T*
(truste I haue deserued no rebuke for my good wyll, euen so I desyre no) Tj T*
(prayse for my payne, cost, and trauell. But faithfullye for the proffyt) Tj T*
(and benyfyt of my countrey I haue don it, that the whole body of the) Tj T*
(Realme may se and vnderstand their leud lyfe and pernitious practisses,) Tj T*
(that all maye spedelye helpe to amend that is amysse. Amen saye all with) Tj T*
(me.) Tj T*
