() Tj T*
(¶ A RUFFLER. Ca. 1.[55] *[leaf 6]*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE Rufflar, because he is first in degre of this odious order: And is) Tj T*
(so called in a statute made for the punishment of Vacabonds, In the) Tj T*
(xxvij. yeare of Kyng Henry the eight, late of most famous memory: Hée) Tj T*
(shall be first placed, as the worthiest of this vnruly rablement. And he) Tj T*
(is so called when he goeth first abroad; eyther he hath serued in the) Tj T*
(warres, or els he hath bene a seruinge man; and, weary of well doing,) Tj T*
(shakinge of all payne, doth chuse him this ydle lyfe, and wretchedly) Tj T*
(wanders aboute the most shyres of this realme. And with stout) Tj T*
(audacyte,[56] demaundeth where he thinketh hée maye be bolde, and) Tj T*
(circomspecte ynough, as he sethe cause to aske charitie, rufully and) Tj T*
(lamentably, that it would make a flyntey hart to relent, and pytie his) Tj T*
(miserable estate, howe he hath bene maymed and broused in the warres;) Tj T*
(_and_, parauenture, some wyll shew you some outward wounde, whiche he) Tj T*
(gotte at some dronken fraye, eyther haltinge of some preuye wounde) Tj T*
(festred with a fylthy firy flankard. For be well assured that the) Tj T*
(hardist souldiers be eyther slayne or maymed, eyther and[57] they escape) Tj T*
(all hassardes, and retourne home agayne, if they bée without reliefe of) Tj T*
(their friends, they wyl surely desperatly robbe and steale, and[58]) Tj T*
(eyther shortlye be hanged or miserably dye in pryson; for they be so) Tj T*
(much ashamed and disdayne to beg or aske charity, that rather they wyll) Tj T*
(as desperatlye fight for to lyue and mayntayne them selues, as manfully) Tj T*
(and valyantly they ventred them selues in the Prynces quarell. Now these) Tj T*
(Rufflars, the out castes of seruing men, when begginge or crauinge) Tj T*
(fayles, then they pycke and pylfer, from other inferiour beggeres that) Tj T*
(they méete by the waye, as Roages, Pallyardes, Mortes, and Doxes. Yea,) Tj T*
(if they méete with a woman alone ridinge to the market, eyther olde man) Tj T*
(or boye, that hée well knoweth wyll not resiste, such they filche and) Tj T*
(spoyle. These rufflars, after a yeare or two at the farthest, become) Tj T*
(vpryght men, vnlesse they be preuented by twind hempe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  {I had of late yeares an old man to my tennant, who customably) Tj T*
(  {a greate tyme went twise in the wéeke to London, eyther) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(wyth fruite or with pescodes, when tyme serued therefore. And as he was) Tj T*
(comminge homewarde on blacke heathe, at the end thereof next to shotars) Tj T*
(hyl, he ouer tooke two rufflars, the one manerly wayting on the other,) Tj T*
(as one had ben the maister, _and_ the other the man or seruant, *[leaf) Tj T*
(6, back]* caryinge his maisteres cloke. this olde man was verye glad) Tj T*
(that hee might haue their company ouer the hyl, because that day he had) Tj T*
(made a good market; for hée had seuen shyllinges in his purse, and a) Tj T*
(nolde angell, which this poore man had thought had not bene in his) Tj T*
(purse, for hée wylled his wyfe ouer night to take out the same angell,) Tj T*
(and laye it vp vntyll his comminge home agayne. And he verely thought) Tj T*
(that his wyfe had so don, whiche in dede for got to do it. Thus after) Tj T*
(salutations had, this maister rufflar entered into co_m_munication with) Tj T*
(this simple olde man, who, ridinge softlye beside them, commoned of many) Tj T*
(matters. Thus fedinge this old man with pleasaunt talke, vntyll they) Tj T*
(weare one the toppe of the hyll, where these rufflares might well) Tj T*
(beholde the coaste about them cleare, Quiclye stepes vnto this poore) Tj T*
