() Tj T*
([Header: HARMAN. A ROGE.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A ROGE. Cap. 4.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A Roge is neither so stoute or hardy as the vpright man. Many of them) Tj T*
(will go fayntly and looke piteously when they sée, either méete any) Tj T*
(person, hauing a kercher, as white as my shooes, tyed about their head,) Tj T*
(with a short staffe in their hand, haltinge, although they nede not,) Tj T*
(requiring almes of such as they méete, or to what house they shal com.) Tj T*
(But you may easely perceiue by their colour _tha_t thei cary both health) Tj T*
(and hipocrisie about them, wherby they get gaine, when others want that) Tj T*
(cannot fayne and dissemble. Others therebee that walke sturdely about) Tj T*
(_th_e cou_n_trey, _and_ faineth to seke a brother or kinsman of his,) Tj T*
(dwelling within som part of _th_e shire;--ether that he hath a letter to) Tj T*
(deliuer to som honest housholder, dwelling out of an other Shyre, and) Tj T*
(will shewe you the same fayre sealed, with the superscription to *[leaf) Tj T*
(9, back]* the partye he speaketh of, because you shall not thinke him to) Tj T*
(runne idelly about the countrey;--either haue they this shyfte, they) Tj T*
(wyll cary a cirtificate or pasport about them from som Iusticer of the) Tj T*
(peace, with his hand and seale vnto the same, howe hée hath bene whipped) Tj T*
(and punished for a vacabonde according to the lawes of this realme, and) Tj T*
(that he muste returne to .T., where he was borne or last dwelt, by a) Tj T*
(certayne daye lymited in the same, whiche shalbe a good longe daye. And) Tj T*
(all this fayned, bycause without feare they woulde wyckedly wander, and) Tj T*
(wyll renue the same where or when it pleasethe them; for they haue of) Tj T*
(their affinity that can wryte and read. These also wyll picke and steale) Tj T*
(as the vpright men, and hath their women and metinges at places) Tj T*
(apoynted, and nothinge to them inferiour in all kynde of knauery. There) Tj T*
(bée of these Roges Curtales, wearinge shorte clokes, that wyll chaunge) Tj T*
(their aparell, as occation seruethe. And their end is eyther hanginge,) Tj T*
(whiche they call trininge in their language, or die miserably of the) Tj T*
(pockes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ There was not long sithens two Roges that alwaies did associate them) Tj T*
(selues together, _and_ would neuer seperat them selues, vnles it were) Tj T*
(for some especiall causes, for they were sworn brothers, _and_ were both) Tj T*
(of one age, and much like of favour: these two, trauelinge into east) Tj T*
(kent, resorted vnto an ale house there,[65] being weried with traueling,) Tj T*
(saluting with short curtisey, when they came into the house, such as) Tj T*
(thei sawe sitting there, in whiche company was the parson of the parish;) Tj T*
(and callinge for a pot of the best ale, sat downe at the tables ende:) Tj T*
(the lykor liked them so well, that they had pot vpon pot, and sometyme,) Tj T*
