([Header: HARMAN. A WYLDE ROGE.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A WYLDE ROGE. Cap. 5.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A Wilde Roge is he that is borne a Roge: he is a more subtil and more) Tj T*
(geuen by nature to all kinde of knauery then the other, as beastely) Tj T*
(begotten in barne or bushes, and from his infancye traded vp in) Tj T*
(trechery; yea, and before ripenes of yeares doth permyt, wallowinge in) Tj T*
(lewde lechery, but that is counted amongest them no sin. For this is) Tj T*
(their custome, that when they mete in barne at night, euery one getteth) Tj T*
(a make[76] to lye wythall, _and_ their chaunce to be twentye in a) Tj T*
(companye, as their is sometyme more and sometyme lesse: for to one man) Tj T*
(that goeth abroad, there are at the least two women, which neuer make it) Tj T*
(straunge when they be called, although she neuer knewe him before. Then) Tj T*
(when the day doth appeare, he rouses him vp, and shakes his eares, and) Tj T*
(awaye wanderinge where he may gette oughte to the hurte of others. Yet) Tj T*
(before he skyppeth oute of hys couche and departeth from his darling, if) Tj T*
(he like her well, he will apoint her where to mete shortlye after, with) Tj T*
(a warninge to worke warely for some chetes, that their meting might be) Tj T*
(the merier.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ Not long sithens, a wild roge chau_n_ced to mete a pore neighbour of) Tj T*
(mine, who for honesty _and_ good natur surmou_n_teth many. This poore) Tj T*
(man, riding homeward from London, where he had made his market, this) Tj T*
(*[leaf 11, back]* roge demaunded a peny for gods sake, to kepe him a) Tj T*
(true man. This simple man, beholding him wel, and sawe he was of taule) Tj T*
(personage with a good quarter staffe in his hand, it much pitied him, as) Tj T*
(he sayd, to se him want; for he was well able to serue his prince in the) Tj T*
(wars. Thus, being moued with pytie, and[77] loked in his pursse to finde) Tj T*
(out a penye; and in loking for the same, he plucked oute viii.) Tj T*
(shyllinges in whyte money, and raked therin to finde a single peny; and) Tj T*
(at the last findinge one, doth offer the same to this wylde roge: but) Tj T*
(he, seinge so much mony in this simple mans hand, being striken to the) Tj T*
(hart with a couetous desire, bid him forth wyth delyuer al that he had,) Tj T*
(or els he woulde with his staffe beat out his braynes. For it was not a) Tj T*
(penye would now quench his thirst, [78]seing so much as he dyd[78]:) Tj T*
(thus, swallowinge his spittell gredely downe, spoyled this poore man of) Tj T*
(al _th_e money that he had, and lept ouer the hedge into a thicke wode,) Tj T*
(and went his waye as merely as this good simple man came home) Tj T*
