([Header: HARMAN. A PRYGGER OF PRAUNCERS.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A PRYGGER OF PRAUNCERS. Cap. 6.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A Prigger of Prauncers be horse stealers; for to prigge signifieth in) Tj T*
(their language to steale, _and_ a Prauncer is a horse: so beinge put) Tj T*
(together, the matter is[79] playne. These go commonly in Ierkins of) Tj T*
(leatherr, or of white frese, _and_ carry litle wands in their hands, and) Tj T*
(will walke through grounds and pastures, to search and se horses meete) Tj T*
(for their purpose. And if thei chau_n_ce to be met and asked by the) Tj T*
(owners of the grounde what they make there, they fayne strayghte that) Tj T*
(they haue loste their waye, and desyre to be enstructed the beste waye) Tj T*
(to such a place. These will also repayre to gentlemens houses and aske) Tj T*
(their charitye, and wyll offer their seruice. And if you aske them what) Tj T*
(they can do, they wyll saye that they can kepe two or thre Geldinges,) Tj T*
(and waite vppon a Gentleman. These haue also their women, that walkinge) Tj T*
(from them in other places, marke where and what they sée abroade, and) Tj T*
(sheweth these Priggars therof when they meete, which is with in a wéeke) Tj T*
(or two. And loke, where they steale any thinge, they conuay _th_e same) Tj T*
(at the least thre score miles of or more.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ There was a Gentleman, a verye friende of myne, rydyng from London) Tj T*
(homewarde into Kente, hauinge with in thrée myles of his house) Tj T*
(busynesse, alyghted of his horse, and his man also, in a pretye *[leaf) Tj T*
(12]* vyllage, where diueres houses were, and looked about hym where he) Tj T*
(myghte haue a conuenient person to walke his horse, because hee would) Tj T*
(speake w_i_t_h_ a Farmer that dwelt on the backe side of the sayde) Tj T*
(village, lytle aboue a quarter of a myle from the place where he) Tj T*
(lighted, and had his man to waight vpon him, as it was mete for his) Tj T*
(callinge: espying a Pryggar there standing, thinking the same to dwell) Tj T*
(there, charging this prity prigginge person to walke his horse well, and) Tj T*
(that they might not stande styll for takyng of colde, and at his returne) Tj T*
(\(which he saide should not be longe\) he would geue hym a peny to drinke,) Tj T*
(and so wente aboute his busines. This peltynge Priggar, proude of his) Tj T*
(praye, walkethe his horse[80] vp and downe tyll he sawe the Gentleman) Tj T*
(out of sighte, and leapes him into the saddell, and awaye he goeth a) Tj T*
(mayne. This Gentleman returninge, and findinge not his horses, sent his) Tj T*
(man to the one end of the vyllage, and he went himselfe vnto the other) Tj T*
