([Header: HARMAN. A PALLYARD.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A PALLYARD. Cap. 7.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THese Palliardes be called also Clapperdogens: these go with patched) Tj T*
(clokes, _and_ haue their Morts with them, which they cal wiues; and if) Tj T*
(he goe to one house, to aske his almes, his wife shall goe to a nother:) Tj T*
(for what they get \(as bread, chéese, malte, and woll\) they sell the same) Tj T*
(for redy money; for so they get more and if they went together. Although) Tj T*
(they be thus[82] deuided in the daie, yet they mete iompe at night. Yf) Tj T*
(they chaunce to come to some gentylmans house standinge *[leaf 12,) Tj T*
(back]* a lone, and be demaunded whether they be man and wyfe, _and_ if) Tj T*
(he perceaue that any doubteth thereof, he sheweth them a Testimonial) Tj T*
(with the ministers name, and others of the same parishe \(naminge a) Tj T*
(parishe in some shere fare distant from the place where he sheweth the) Tj T*
(same\). This writing he carieth to salue that sore. Ther be many Irishe) Tj T*
(men that goe about with cou_n_terfeate licenses; and if they perceiue) Tj T*
(you wil straytly examen them, they will immediatly saye they can speake) Tj T*
(no Englishe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ Farther, vnderstand for trouth that the worst and wickedst of all this) Tj T*
(beastly generation are scarse comparable to these prating Pallyardes.) Tj T*
(All for _th_e most parte of these wil either lay to their legs an herb) Tj T*
(called Sperewort, eyther Arsnicke, which is called Ratesbane. The nature) Tj T*
(of this Spereworte wyll rayse a great blister in a night vpon the) Tj T*
(soundest part of his body; and if the same be taken away, it wyl dry vp) Tj T*
(againe and no harme. But this Arsnicke will so poyson the same legge or) Tj T*
