(*[leaf 15]* ¶ A COUNTERFET CRANKE. Cap. 11.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THese that do counterfet the Cranke be yong knaues and yonge harlots,) Tj T*
(that depely dissemble the falling sicknes. For the Cranke in their) Tj T*
(language is the falling euyll. I haue séene some of these with fayre) Tj T*
(writinges testimoniall, with the names and seales of some men of worshyp) Tj T*
(in Shropshyre, and in other Shieres farre of, that I haue well knowne,) Tj T*
(and haue taken the same from them. Many of these do go without) Tj T*
(writinges, and wyll go halfe naked, and looke most pitiously. And if any) Tj T*
(clothes be geuen them, the[y][95] immediatly sell the same, for weare it) Tj T*
(they wyll not, because they would bée the more pitied, and weare fylthy) Tj T*
(clothes on their heades, and neuer go without a péece of whyte sope) Tj T*
(about them, which, if they sée cause or present gaine, they wyll priuely) Tj T*
(conuey the same into their mouth, and so worke the same there, that they) Tj T*
(wyll fome as it were a Boore, _and_ maruelously for a tyme torment them) Tj T*
(selues; and thus deceiue they the common people, and gayne much. These) Tj T*
(haue commonly their harlots as the other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Apon Alhollenday in the morning last Anno domini. 1566, or my[96] booke) Tj T*
(was halfe printed, I meane the first impression, there came earely in) Tj T*
(the morninge a Counterfet Cranke vnder my lodgynge at the whyte Fryares,) Tj T*
(wythin the cloyster, in a lyttle yard or coorte, where aboutes laye two) Tj T*
(or thre great Ladyes, beyng without the lyberties of London, where by he) Tj T*
(hoped for the greatter gayne; this Cranke there lamentably lamentinge) Tj T*
(and pitefully crying to be releued, declared to dyuers their hys) Tj T*
(paynfull and miserable dysease. I being rysen and not halfe ready, harde) Tj T*
(his dolfull wordes and rufull mornings, hering him name the falling) Tj T*
(sicknes, thought assuredlye to my selfe that hée was a depe desemblar;) Tj T*
(so, comminge out at a sodayne, and beholdinge his vgly and yrksome) Tj T*
(attyre, hys lothsome and horyble countinance, it made me in a meruelous) Tj T*
(parplexite what to thinke of hym, whether it were fayned or trouth,--for) Tj T*
(after this manner went he: he was naked from the wast vpward, sauyng he) Tj T*
(had a old Ierken[97] of leather patched, and that was lose[98] about) Tj T*
(hym, that all his bodye laye out bare; a filthy foule cloth he ware on) Tj T*
(his head, being cut for the purpose, hauing a narowe place to put out) Tj T*
(his face, with a bauer made to trusse vp his beard, and a stryng that) Tj T*
(tyed the same downe close aboute his necke; with an olde felt hat which) Tj T*
(he styll caried in his hande to receaue the charytye and deuotion of the) Tj T*
(people, for that woulde he hold out from hym; hauyng hys face, from the) Tj T*
(eyes downe ward, all smerd with freshe bloud, *[leaf 15, back]* as) Tj T*
(thoughe he had new falen, and byn tormented wyth his paynefull) Tj T*
(panges,--his Ierken beinge all be rayde with durte and myre, and hys) Tj T*
(hatte and hosen also, as thoughe hée hadde wallowed in the myre: sewerly) Tj T*
(the sighte was monstrous and terreble. I called hym vnto me, and) Tj T*
(demaunded of hym what he ayled. "A, good maister," quoth he, "I haue the) Tj T*
(greuous and paynefull dyseas called the falynge syckenes." "Why," quoth) Tj T*
(I, "howe commeth thy Ierken, hose, and hat so be rayd with durte and) Tj T*
(myre, and thy skyn also?" "A, good master, I fell downe on the backesyde) Tj T*
(here in the fowle lane harde by the watersyde; and there I laye all most) Tj T*
(all night, and haue bled all most all the bloude owte in my bodye." It) Tj T*
