([Header: HARMAN. A DOMMERAR.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A DOMMERAR. Cap. 12.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THese Dommerars are leud and most subtyll people: the moste part of) Tj T*
(these are Walch men, and wyll neuer speake, vnlesse they haue extreame) Tj T*
(punishment, but wyll gape, and with a maruelous force wyll hold downe) Tj T*
(their toungs doubled, groning for your charyty, and holding vp their) Tj T*
(handes full pitiously, so that with their déepe dissimulation they get) Tj T*
(very much. There are of these many, _and_ but one that I vnderstand of) Tj T*
(hath lost his toung in dede. Hauing on a time occasion to ride to) Tj T*
(Dartforde, to speake with a priest there, who maketh all kinde of) Tj T*
(conserues very well, and vseth stilling of waters; And repayringe to his) Tj T*
(house, I founde a Dommerar at his doore, and the priest him selfe) Tj T*
(perusinge his[118] lycence, vnder the seales and hands of certayne) Tj T*
(worshypfull men, had[119] thought the same to be good and effectuall. I) Tj T*
(taking the same writing, and reading it ouer, and noting the seales,) Tj T*
(founde one of the seales like vnto a seale that I had aboute me, which) Tj T*
(seale I bought besides Charing crosse, that I was out of doubte it was) Tj T*
(none of those Gentlemens seales that had sub[s]cribed. And hauing) Tj T*
(vnderstanding before of their peuish practises, made me to conceaue that) Tj T*
(all was forged and nought. I made the more hast home; for well I wyst) Tj T*
(that he would and must of force passe through the parysh where I dwelt;) Tj T*
(for there was no other waye for hym. And comminge homewarde, I found) Tj T*
(them in the towne, accordinge to my expectation, where they were staid;) Tj T*
(for there was a Pallyarde associate with the Dommerar and partaker of) Tj T*
