() Tj T*
(¶ A SWADDER, OR PEDLER. Cap. 14.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THese Swadders and Pedlers bee not all euyll, but of an indifferent) Tj T*
(behauiour. These stand in great awe of the vpright men, for they haue) Tj T*
(often both wares and money of them. But for as much as they seeke gayne) Tj T*
(vnlawfully against the lawes and statutes of this noble realme, they are) Tj T*
(well worthy to be registred among the number of vacabonds; and) Tj T*
(vndoubtedly I haue hadde some of them brought before me, when I was in) Tj T*
(commission of the peace, as malefactors, for bryberinge and stealinge.) Tj T*
(And nowe of late it is a greate practes of the vpright man, when he hath) Tj T*
(gotten a botye, to bestowe the same vpon a packefull of wares, and so) Tj T*
(goeth a time for his pleasure, because he would lyue with out suspition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A IARKE MAN, AND A PATRICO. Cap. 15.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(FOR as much as these two names, a Iarkeman and a Patrico, bée in the old) Tj T*
(briefe of vacabonds, and set forth as two kyndes of euil doers, you) Tj T*
(shall vnderstande that a Iarkeman hathe his name of a Iarke, which is a) Tj T*
(seale in their Language, as one should make writinges and set seales for) Tj T*
(lycences and pasporte[123]. And for trouth there is none that goeth) Tj T*
(aboute the countrey of them that can eyther wryte so good and fayre a) Tj T*
(hand, either indite so learnedly, as I haue sene _and_ handeled a number) Tj T*
(of them: but haue the same made in good townes where they come, as what) Tj T*
(can not be hadde for money, as the prouerbe sayth \("_Omnia venalia) Tj T*
(Rome_"\), and manye hath confessed the same to me. *[leaf 18, back]* Now,) Tj T*
(also, there is a Patrico, and not a Patriarcho[124], whiche in their) Tj T*
(language is a priest that should make mariages tyll death dyd depart;) Tj T*
(but they haue none such, I am well assured; for I put you out of doubt) Tj T*
(that not one amo[n]gest a hundreth of them are maried, for they take) Tj T*
(lechery for no sinne, but naturall fellowshyp and good lyking loue: so) Tj T*
(that I wyll not blot my boke with these two that be not.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Header: HARMAN. A DEMAUNDER FOR GLYMMAR.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A DEMAUNDER FOR GLYMMAR. Cap. 16.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THese Demaunders for glymmar be for the moste parte wemen; for glymmar,) Tj T*
(in their language, is fyre. These goe with fayned[125] lycences and) Tj T*
(counterfayted wrytings, hauing the hands and seales of suche gentlemen) Tj T*
(as dwelleth nere to the place where they fayne them selues to haue bene) Tj T*
(burnt, and their goods consumed with fyre. They wyll most) Tj T*
(lamentable[126] demaunde your charitie, _and_ wyll quicklye shed salte) Tj T*
ET
