([Header: HARMAN. A DOXE.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ A DOXE. Cap. 20.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THese Doxes be broken and spoyled of their maydenhead by the vpright) Tj T*
(men, and then they haue their name of Doxes, and not afore. And) Tj T*
(afterwarde she is commen and indifferent for any that wyll vse her, as) Tj T*
(_homo_ is a commen name to all men. Such as be fayre and some what) Tj T*
(handsome, kepe company with the walkinge Mortes, and are redye alwayes) Tj T*
(for the vpright men, and are cheifely mayntayned by them, for others) Tj T*
(shalbe spoyled for their sakes: the other, inferior, sort wyll resorte) Tj T*
(to noble mens places, and gentlemens houses, standing at the gate,) Tj T*
(eyther lurkinge on the backesyde about backe houses, eyther in hedge) Tj T*
(rowes, or some other thycket, expectinge their praye, which is for the) Tj T*
(vncomely company of some curteous gest, of whome they be refreshed with) Tj T*
(meate and some money, where eschaunge is made, ware for ware: this bread) Tj T*
(and meate they vse to carrye in their *[leaf 24]* greate hosen; so that) Tj T*
(these beastlye brybinge[150] bréeches serue manye tymes for bawdye) Tj T*
(purposes. I chaunced, not longe sithens, familiarly to commen with a) Tj T*
(Doxe that came to my gate, and surelye a pleasant harlot, and not so) Tj T*
(pleasant as wytty, and not so wytty as voyd of all grace and goodnes. I) Tj T*
(founde, by her talke, that shée hadde passed her tyme lewdlye eyghttene) Tj T*
(yeares in walkinge aboute. I thoughte this a necessary instrument to) Tj T*
(attayne some knowledge by; and before I woulde grope her mynde, I made) Tj T*
(her both to eate and drynke well; that done, I made her faythfull) Tj T*
(promisse to geue her some money, yf she would open and dyscouer to me) Tj T*
(such questions as I woulde demaunde of her, and neuer to bée wraye her,) Tj T*
(neither to disclose her name. "And you shoulde," sayth she, "I were) Tj T*
(vndon:" "feare not that," quoth I; "but, I praye the," quoth I, "say) Tj T*
(nothing but trouth." "I wyll not," sayth shée. "Then, fyrste tell me,") Tj T*
(quoth I, "how many vpright men and Roges dost thou knowe, or hast thou) Tj T*
(knowne and byn conuersaunt with, and what their names be?" She paused a) Tj T*
(whyle, and sayd, "why do you aske me, or wherefore?" "For nothinge els,") Tj T*
(as I sayde, "but that I woulde knowe them when they came to my gate.") Tj T*
("Nowe, by my trouth" \(quoth she\) "then are yea neuer the neare, for all) Tj T*
(myne acquayntaunce, for the moste parte, are deade." "Dead!" quoth I,) Tj T*
("howe dyed they, for wante of cherishinge, or of paynefull diseases?") Tj T*
(Then she sighed, and sayde they were hanged. "What, all?" quoth I, "and) Tj T*
(so manye walke abroade, as I dayelye see?" "By my trouth," quoth she, "I) Tj T*
(knowe not paste six or seuen by their names," and named the same to me.) Tj T*
("When were they hanged?" quoth I. "Some seuen yeares a gone, some thrée) Tj T*
(yeares, and some w_i_t_h_in this fortnight," and declared the place) Tj T*
(where they weare executed, which I knewe well to bée true, by the report) Tj T*
ET
