(         *That is very good for vs.*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    Now we haue well bousd, let vs strike some chete.) Tj T*
(    *Nowe we haue well dronke, let us steale some thinge.*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    Yonder dwelleth a quyere cuffen, it were beneship to myll hym.) Tj T*
(    *Yonder dwelleth a hoggeshe and choyrlyshe man, it were very well) Tj T*
(    donne to robbe him.*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    ROGE. Nowe bynge we a waste to the hygh pad, the ruffmanes is by.) Tj T*
(          Naye, let vs go hence to the hygh waye, the wodes is at hand.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    MAN. So may we happen on the Harmanes, and cly the Iarke, or to the) Tj T*
(    quyerken and skower quyaer cramprings, and so to tryning on the) Tj T*
(    chates.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    *[leaf 29, back] So we maye chaunce to set in the stockes, eyther) Tj T*
(    be whypped, eyther had to prison house, and there be shackled with) Tj T*
(    bolttes and fetters, and then to hange on the gallowes.*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    Gerry gan, the ruffian clye thee.) Tj T*
(    *A torde in thy mouth, the deuyll take thee.*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    MAN. What, stowe your bene, cofe, and cut benat whydds, and byng we) Tj T*
(    to rome vyle, to nyp a bong; so shall we haue lowre for the bousing) Tj T*
(    ken, and when we byng back to the deuseauyel, we wyll fylche some) Tj T*
(    duddes of the Ruffemans, or myll the ken for a lagge of dudes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    *What, holde your peace, good fellowe, and speake better wordes, and) Tj T*
(    go we to London, to cut a purse; then shal we haue money for the ale) Tj T*
(    house, and when wee come backe agayne into the country, wee wyll) Tj T*
(    steale some lynnen clothes of one[173] hedges, or robbe some house) Tj T*
(    for a bucke of clothes.*) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ By this lytle ye maye holy and fully vnderstande their vntowarde talke) Tj T*
(and pelting speache, mynglede without measure; and as they haue begonne) Tj T*
(of late to deuyse some new termes for certien thinges, so wyll they in) Tj T*
(tyme alter this, and deuyse as euyll or worsse. This language nowe) Tj T*
(beinge knowen and spred abroade, yet one thinge more I wyll ad vnto, not) Tj T*
(meaninge to Englyshe the same, because I learned the same[174] of a) Tj T*
(shameles Doxe, but for the phrase of speche I set it forth onely.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There was a proude patrico and a nosegent, he tooke his Iockam in his) Tj T*
(famble, and a wappinge he went, he dokte the Dell, hee pryge to praunce,) Tj T*
(he byngd a waste into the darke mans, he fylcht the Cofe, with out any) Tj T*
(fylch man.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Header: HARMAN. NYCHOLAS BLUNTE'S TRICKS.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(WHyle this second Impression was in printinge, it fortuned that Nycholas) Tj T*
(Blunte, who called hym selfe Nycholan Gennyns, a counterefet Cranke,) Tj T*
(that is spoken of in this booke, was fonde begging in the whyte fryers) Tj T*
