(*[leaf 3]* THE VISITER.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An honest youth, not many yeares since, seruant in this City, had leaue) Tj T*
(of his master at whitsontide to see his friends, who dwelt some fifty) Tj T*
(miles from London. It hapned at a Country wake, his mother and hee came) Tj T*
(acquainted with a precise scholler, that, vnder colour of strickt life,) Tj T*
(hath bin reputed for that hee is not: hee is well knowen in Paules) Tj T*
(Churchyard, and hath beene lately a visiting in Essex; for so he) Tj T*
(presumes to tearme his cosening walks: and therefore wee will call him) Tj T*
(here a Visiter. This honest seeming man must needes \(sith his iourney) Tj T*
(lay to London\) stay at the yong mans mothers all the holy daies: where) Tj T*
(as on his desert hee was kindly vsed; at length, the young man, hauing) Tj T*
(receiued his mother's blessing, with other his friendes giftes,) Tj T*
(amounting to some ten poundes, was to this hypocrite as to a faithful) Tj T*
(guide committed, and toward London they ride: by the way this Visiter) Tj T*
(discourses how excellent insight he had in Magick, to recouer by Art) Tj T*
(anything lost or stolne. Well, to sant Albons they reach; there they sup) Tj T*
(together, and, after the carowsing of some quarts of wine, they go to) Tj T*
(bed, where they kindly sleepe,--the Visiter slily, but the young man) Tj T*
(soundly. Short tale to make--out of his bed-fellow's sleeue this Visiter) Tj T*
(conuaid his twenty Angels, besides some other od siluer, hid it closely,) Tj T*
(and so fell to his rest. Morning comes--vp gets this couple--immediately) Tj T*
(the money was mist, much adoo was made; the Chamberlaine with sundry) Tj T*
(other seruants examined; and so hot the contention, that the good man,) Tj T*
(for the discharge of his house, was sending for a Constable to haue them) Tj T*
(both first searcht, his seruants Chests after. In the meane time the) Tj T*
(Visiter cals the yong man aside, and bids him neuer grieue, but take) Tj T*
(horse; and he warrants him, ere they be three miles out of towne, to) Tj T*
(helpe him to his money by Art, saying:--"In these Innes ye see how we) Tj T*
(shall be out-faced, and, beeing vnknowne, how euer we be wrongd, get) Tj T*
(little remedy." The yong man, in good hope, desired him to pay the) Tj T*
(reckoning, which done, together they ride. Being some two miles from the) Tj T*
(towne, they ride out of the ordinary way: there he tels this youth how) Tj T*
(vnwilling hee was to enter into the action, but that it was lost in his) Tj T*
(company, and so forth. Well, a Circle was made, wondrous words were) Tj T*
(vsed, many muttrings made: at length hee cries out,--"vnder a greene) Tj T*
(turfe, by the East side of an Oake; goe thither, goe thither." This) Tj T*
(thrice he cryed so ragingly, as the yuong man gest him mad, and was with) Tj T*
(feare almost beside himself. At length, pausing, quoth this Visiter,) Tj T*
("heard ye nothing cry?" "Cry!" said the yong man, "yes; *[leaf 5, back]*) Tj T*
