(to provide good lodgings for such persons. Hence two St. Julian's) Tj T*
(crosses, in saltier, are in chief of the innholders' arms, and the old) Tj T*
(motto was:--'When I was harbourless, ye lodged me.' This benevolent) Tj T*
(attention to travellers procured him the epithet of 'the good) Tj T*
(herbergeor,' and in France '_bon herbet_.' His legend in a MS.,) Tj T*
(Bodleian, 1596, fol. 4, alludes to this:--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    'Therefore yet to this day, thei that over lond wende,) Tj T*
(    They biddeth Seint Julian, anon, that gode herborw he hem sende;) Tj T*
(    And Seint Julianes Pater Noster ofte seggeth also) Tj T*
(    For his faders soule, and his moderes, that he hem bring therto.') Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And in '_Le dit des Heureux_,' an old French fabliau:--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    'Tu as dit la patenotre) Tj T*
(    Saint Julian à cest matin,) Tj T*
(    Soit en Roumans, soit en Latin;) Tj T*
(    Or tu seras bien ostilé.') Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In mediæval French, _L'hotel Saint Julien_ was synonymous with good) Tj T*
ET
